	abstract = {This chapter examines how hierarchical position shapes cognition and sensemaking in organisations. Drawing on sensemaking theory, the attention-based view, stratified systems theory, and the psychology of power, the authors develop a framework demonstrating how interpretation varies across executive, middle management, and operational levels. Eight mechanisms are identified: differential information access, scope of responsibility, temporal orientation, identity, social reference groups, risk perception, information distortion, and divergent mental models. Implications for strategic alignment, implementation failure, and decision quality are examined, followed by intervention principles across structural, process, communication, and cultural domains. Boundary conditions addressing organisational context, individual differences, and cross-cultural factors are specified. The chapter contributes theoretical advancement and practical guidance for diagnosing vertical sensemaking challenges and designing effective interventions. Copyright © 2027, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. Copying or distributing in print or electronic forms without written permission of IGI Global Scientific Publishing is prohibited. Use of this chapter to train generative artificial intelligence (AI) technologies is expressly prohibited. The publisher reserves all rights to license its use for generative AI training and machine learning model development.},
